<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thurday&nbsp;8th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
<B>AutoDelta</B> phoned me this morning. Jano said that if the car was an Alfa
Romeo then there would be no problem. Although very helpful they did not seem
interested in performing the Tipo conversion themselves. However they did
say that for 2.0 litre 4
cylinder Alfa Romeo they use 0.55 bar of boost which gives 220 bhp and 220 lbs/ft
of torque.
<P>
<B>Bob Brain Developments</B> phoned in afternoon. Having read through my letter
they thought the best approach would be to use an Integrale engine, wiring loom
and ECU. They know someone who would be able to make up a radiator, oil cooler
and intercooler. They will phone over the weekend once they have had more chance
to investigate further.
<P>
<B>Tanc Barrett</B> phoned in afternoon and they were very helpful. Something I had
not realized was that the engine number determines whether the car needs a catalytic
converter and it is not simply a matter of all post August 1992 cars. See the
<A HREF="../faq/faq.html#catalytic_converter">FAQ</A> section for more details.
<P> They were concerned that the wiring dirgram used by Alex did not show any
connections for the electronic boost control. This controls the waste gate actuation
based on a map in the ECU. They prefered the engine to be an Integrale since the
ECU is easier to tune and they have a variety of chips to suite. They said that
without any internal engine modifications, simply by chipping the engine they
can produce between 260 and 280 bhp using 1.5 bar of boost. They would hope to keep
the existing wiring loom and use a new ECU, patching in the other wires providing
the existing pin put is compatible. They estimated about 10 days to do the work
costing about &pound;2000 in labour alone. We discussed sourcing various types of
engines including a brand new Integrale one for about &pound;2850 (excluding turbo).
A Thema Series 3 engine (CAT) would be very difficult to track down as only 27 cars
using the engine were imported into the UK.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp;13th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I contacted Alex and he said he had made a mistake in the wiring diagram for the
Integrale. The RPM Amplifier was actually the electronic boost controller.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp;14th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Sent another couple of letters off to:
