</TD>
</TR> 


<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thurday&nbsp;15th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
A week later and no other replies so phoned up several garages to check they had
recieved the letter.
<P><B>Evolution Engineering</B> had received the letter but were not interested
in the project as it would take up too much time.

<P><B>Van Aaken Developments Ltd</B> said they had not received the letter but would
not be interested anyway as it was not a Seicento or Cinquecento.

<P><B>Omicron Engineering Ltd</B> has recieved the letter and were in the middle of
writing a two page response. Their main line of business is older Lancia's from
1950 though to 1970 and restoring cars. However they do have Turbo experience as their
boss worked on the Lotus Turbo engine.
<P>
They were concerned as to whether the Tipo gearbox would be up to the job. It might be
best to use the Thema gearbox as an Integrale one would be
4WD.
<P>
The Series 3 Thema turbo engine is tuned more for refinement, coming from
a luxury car, and developed
205 bhp and 224 lbs/ft of torque using a Bosch ignition system. The Integrale
EVO 1 engine has a more sporty tune and develops 210 bhp and 224 lbs/ft of torque.
The advantage of the Integrale engine is that it also uses the Webber IAW
ignition system which is the same as the Tipo.
<P>
They said the big problem with costing the transplant is the number of unknowns. They
know it took Alex's garage 10 days to do the work but how many people were working
on it and for how many hours? The electrics might be a problem as they are not
electrical engineers although they know how it all works. A warrenty on a
second hand engine would be difficult to
supply without them stripping it down and rebuilding it, although their work would
be covered. They would also be able to obtain a new integrale engine however this
would most likely have been sitting around for years and would need checking over.
<P>
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Sunday&nbsp;18th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received email from Alex containing details about how many people worked on his
conversion and for how many hours.

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp;19th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received a detailed <A HREF="omicron.html">letter</A> from <B>Omicron Engineering Ltd</B> which was the
sort of response I was hoping for from everopne else.

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp;20th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received a more detailed <A HREF="vanaaken.html">reply</A> from <B>Van Aaken</B>.
Received email from Alex explaining how they had used a Cossie ECU.
Sent another letter off to:
<P>
   <TABLE BORDER=1 CELLPADDING="4">
   <TR>
   <TD VALIGN=TOP>
   <B>Avanti&nbsp;Motorsport&nbsp;International</B><BR>
   84 West End Street<BR>
   Somerset<BR>
   BA16 0LP<BR>
   <P>
   Tel:&nbsp;01458&nbsp;446517<BR>
   <A HREF="http://freespace.virgin.net/avanti.motorsport/index2.htm" target="_blank">Web Site</A>
   </TD>
   </TR></TABLE>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp;23th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received another email off Alex. He said after reading the progress so far some
things needed clearing up. He says that all the Fiat/Lancia 2 litre 16V turbo engines
are physically the same in that they have the same pistons, crank, head, rods etc.
He said that Van Aaken were wrong saying that the Integrale engine won't fit
because it's 4wd. As I thought this does not make any difference, it's only the
gearbox that would be different. Alex says that he thinks the best engine is the
Fiat Coupe because you can find newer engines (up until 1996) and the Coupe is
built on the Tipo platform so it uses the same mounting points.
This engine is also used in the new Delta and in the Kappa. He gones on to say
that there is no connection between the engine and the injection system.
The Fiat Coupe engine can work with the Integrale injection system or even the
Cossie (after a remap of the ECU). The Tipo gearbox copes well with the abuse
and if it fails for some unknown reason (although there are no signs for this
on his car) I could always fit the coupe gearbox or possibly the coupe 20v
turbo 6 speed gearbox.
</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp;26th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Chased up a few more garages. <B>Aladdin Motorsport</B> are hopefully going
to put something together by the weekend for me.
<P>
Peter Ward from <B>Racing Technologies</B> phoned and I was very impressed with
what he had to say. After half an hour on the phone I began to realize that
I just don't think I can afford the quality of work that
they produce. They specialize in bespoke solutions just like what I have in
mind. However they usually work with an unlimited budget on Ferraris, Aston
Martins, Porsches, Integrales, Skylines etc. Their workspoke according to Nareman
is more like an operating theatre. The work is fully guarenteed and to extremely
high quality. The engine would be made to look like it was original.
<P>
Peter was very concerned about the potential of the car after the conversion.
250 bhp can be achived without chipping, just by good engineering. For example
intercooler size and placement or the exhaust system.
280 bhp and 280 lbs/ft torque would easily be achiveable however
this would give the car 160+mph top speed. Brakes, suspension and airflow become
important then. He would not let the car out of the workshop without these being
addressed since he would be liable due to knowing that
he had sold a car that was dangerous. We talked about brake conversions,
possibly fitting 4 pot calipers and discs off a Fiat Coupe. Soemthing which
I had in mind anyway. Traction control would be needed so hubs fitted with ABS
sensors would be necessary. If I did not want this work carrying out
in one go we talked about limiting the boost or fitting a restrictor to
reduce power until the car was ready for it.
<P>
Peter said he could not help me anymore without some idea of a budget from me. However
having heard him talk about &pound;30,000 conversions on Nissan Skylines I'm
a little bit nervous about mentioning a budget of 6k including engine! I do
however have every confidence that their work would be the best bar none.
</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp;27th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Steve Smith the owner of <B>Walkers Garage</B> phoned.
They have not done a conversion like this before however they said that
the physical engine size is similar and don't see a problem. They would
prefer to replace the whole ignition wiring loom and ECU rather than tap into
the Tipo's for reliablilty. They would prefer to use an Integrale engine
and have several in various conditions currently lying around. &pound;3300 + VAT
for new one, &pound;2500 + VAT for reconditioned and &pound;1500 - &pound;1750 + VAT
for 2nd hand. These prices don't include a turbo.
<P>
They have lots of parts lying around that they have accumulated over time
such as wiring looms, ECU, chips, pipework, radiators, intercoolers.
<P>
They said it would be best to place the intercooler alongside radiator rather
than behind as this will ensure it gets a good supply of cold air rather
than that which has just been heated up by water radiator.
They would hopefully be able to use the Tipo's radiator as the conection points
appear to be in a similar place.
<P>
The exhaust on integrale takes a slightly different route under engine, to one side
rather than straight underneath but it should be possible to have a pipe made up to fit.
Really it's just a matter of tackling each problem when they come to it which
makes it difficult to provide an accurate quote. Estimated labour would be in
the region of &pound;1500 + VAT which is about 50 hours.
<P>
The standard turbo on earlier Integrales is a Garrett T3 which normally
runs 1 bar of boost. The later catayltic converter models had a T34b which is
smaller and with less lag. Although if the car was to be tuned they would
prefer to use the T3 as the T34b would probably overspeed and explode.
<P>
I asked them about the age of engines and whether the engine would need a
catayltic converter. They said they would leave the legal issues up to me
but the engine number is very difficult to see at it's behind the turbo
by an alloy can which is part of the crank case breather system
<P>
They have chips to increase power to 250 bhp and add 20% extra torque although
they said I would be more than happy with the standard 210 bhp
<P>
Later in the day Steve spoke to me again with a rough estimate of how much the
complete job would take.
<P>
<TABLE BORDER=0>
<TR><TD>&pound;2500</TD><TD>&nbsp;&nbsp;</TD><TD>Reconditioned engine</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>&pound;500</TD><TD>&nbsp;&nbsp;</TD><TD>Reconditioned turbo</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>&pound;1000</TD><TD>&nbsp;&nbsp;</TD><TD>Intercooler, oil cooler, ECU, wiring loom, pipework</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>&pound;1500</TD><TD>&nbsp;&nbsp;</TD><TD>6 days ~ 50 hrs labour</TD></TR>
<TR><TD>&pound;125</TD><TD>&nbsp;&nbsp;</TD><TD>New clutch, uprated 30% over standard Integrale.</TD></TR>
<TR><TD COLSPAN=3><CENTER>Total &pound;5625 + VAT = &pound;6600</CENTER></TD></TR>
</TABLE>
<P>
They would be able to start the work the beginning of April.
</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp;28th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I spoke to George Roberts in the evening about my Turbo conversion. George has dismantled
several Tipo 16V and Fiat Coupe 16V cars and says that they are very similar. Pretty much
the same car just a different body. Even the exhaust on the Coupe should fit the
tipo all the way to the mid box between the rear wheels.
<P>
He thinks the the best route would be to buy
a scrapped Coupe 16V Turbo and remove the engine complete with it's wiring loom.
Drop the engine into the Tipo (it will use the same mounting points), pass the ECU
though the bulkhead and then plumb in the engine. He saids to make sure you get
both the ECU and the RED key with the engine. The key contains a transponder which disables
the ECU's immobiliser allowing
the engine to start. The RED key is the master key allowing other BLUE keys to be
cut from it. Don't loose the RED key or buy a Coupe without it since if you
loose the key you will need to buy a new ECU and corresponding key which together are
in the region of &pound;1000.
<P>
I sent an email to <B>Evolution Engineering</B> asking about which turbos were
fitted to various Integrale engines. I also asked which would be best to use and
how much I should expect to pay for one.
<P>
I send an email to <B>Tanc Barrett</B> also asking about which turbos were fitted
to various Integrale engines. Asked them to confirm which engine they would prefer
to use and a price for a new and reconditioned engine of that type. Also asked
whether they would prefer to replace the wiring loom or modify the Tipo's.
</TD>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>March 2001</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp;1st</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Sent an email to <B>Racing Technologies</B> detailing my budget for the engine
conversion. It also contained details of future brake and suspension upgrades I
was thinking of to allay Peter's fears that the car would be dangerous without
these areas being addressed.
<P>
Got <A HREF="evoengineering1.html">reply</A> back from <B>Evolution Engineering</B> regarding which turbos were
fitted to which cars and prices.
<P>
I replied querying how the smaller turbos fitted to later models produced more
power. A smaller turbo would be better as there is less lag but would it produce
the 250 bhp I wanted? I also asked whether they would reconsider doing the work.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp;2nd</TD>
<TD>
Recieved <A HREF="evoengineering2.html">reply</A> from <B>Evolution Engineering</B>.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp;3rd</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Nareman Virk visted <B>Racing Technologies</B> to talk about his suspension. Whilst
there Narman memtioned my Turbo Conversion. Peter Ward said that he would not be able
to start the work until August at the earliest due to his commitments to the
racing season. He also charges &pound;50 a hour which is &pound;2000 a week. He
advised against converting an engine to use a Turbo as using an engine already
designed for a Turbo is much better.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp;5th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Sent another copy of original letter to <B>John Whalley Ltd</B> and <B>
L&amp;M International Ltd</B> as they did not receive it.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp;7th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Read a bit more about Turbos on the Evo Forum to try and find out which size is best.
Apparently the highest flow rate for the Integrale Evo II turbo (smaller) is about 24 lb/min of air
which translates into about 260 bhp, but above 245 bhp the turbo is working far
from its efficiency range. With an Evo I turbo (larger) the limit is about 300-305 bhp,
so 270-280 bhp are good figures. 270 bhp might be possible from the smaller turbo
until it fails due to overspeed.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp;8th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
L&amp;M phoned and sounded interested in working on the project.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp;12th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received letter back from <A HREF="whalley.html">John Whalley</A>
explaining that they are not interested in working on the project. They also
express their concerns about the positioning of the intercooler.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Sunday&nbsp;18th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Decided that I could do the transplant myself. Most companies that I'd contacted
said they were not interested. Those that were interested could not give any
guarentee of the work and wanted a large amount of money. What I'm going to do is
look out for a crashed Fiat Coupe 16V Turbo. Use it's engine, turbo, exhaust,
gearbox, intercooler, oil cooler and ignition wiring loom. The transplant should be
relatively easy which is why I can't understand why the various companies want to
charge so much labour. The engine is identical apart from the turbo bolted on the
front.
<P>
The Tipo's ignition wiring loom will be thrown away and replaced with the one from
the Coupe. The one is my car is looking rather tired anyway as most of the connection
boots have split. The gearbox is being replaced as the gears are probably different and
more likely to be designed to work with the Turbo. It is also a cable operated
gearbox which has a nicer feel.
<P>
There should be lots of spare parts off the Tipo for sale afterwards such as:
<UL>
<LI>A complete 3dr Supersprint exhaust system with cat replacement pipe</LI>
<LI>A 3dr exhaust manifold</LI>
<LI>A set of good quality ignition leads</LI>
<LI>A starter motor</LI>
<LI>An alternator</LI>
<LI>A gearbox</LI>
<LI>A Catalytic converter</LI>
<LI>A Flexihose exhaust section</LI>
<LI>An ignition module and coil</LI>
</UL>
<P>
Inquired about a traction control system. A company called
<A HREF="http://www.racelogic.co.uk" target="_blank">Race Logic</A> produce one.
However the system costs &pound;495+VAT and requires all 4 wheels to have ABS sensors
fitted which mine does not. Also inquired about Samco uprated hoses. They do a set of
5 or 6 hoses for about &pound;120.22 + VAT. This maybe something I think about later
but is not important in order to get the car back on the road.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>April 2001</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp;12th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I now understand a bit more about the way the Fiat Coupe ECU works.
There are two types of ECU,
one with the CODE key system and one without the CODE key system. Both use the same
ignition wiring loom. A car fitted with a CODE key ECU can have it removed and
fitted with a non CODE key system ECU. The car will now work without the transponder
key. The only problem being that all cars sold in the UK had CODE key ECUs. I
therefore have to make sure that the donor car has a key and if possible the red
master key. The Coupe ECU is definitly different from the one used in the Tipo 16V.
</TD>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp;13th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Probably not the best of dates to remove the engine! George is helping me with the
conversion as amongst other things he knows an awful lot about stripping down Tipos and Coupes. He arrived
at 7:15am and we started work on the car at 7:30am. Probably much to the annoyance
of the neighbours!.
<P>
With the car outside the battery was removed together with
it's plastic catch tray and the ignition module and coil. We then removed
the bumper (6x13mm bolts), disconnected the fog lights lead and front grill (two
screws and plastic clips). We then tried
to remove the slam panel however the two locating bolts holding
it onto the wings were slipping so we had to remove the headlights (4x8mm bolts)
and grip the nuts through the holes left. The bonnect lock was disconnected from
the to of the slam panel. The radiator was removed and finally the slam panel could be
moved out of the way.
<P>
All wiring connectors
were removed from the engine and carefully labeled using sticky labels.
All coolant and oil breather pipes leading off the engine were removed.
The gear linkage was disconnected by undoing the 3 bolts holding the arms to the gear
box. This turned out to be a mistake as I had left the car in gear and we needed to
reconnect them to put it in neutral! The clutch actuator can be removed by simply undoing the
two bolts holding the slave cylinder to the top of the gearbox, you don't need to
disconnect the hydraulic pipes. The alternator was loosened and removed together with
it's belt.
<P>
The exhaust manifold bolts were undone and the front wheel nuts loosened. That
was all we could do before jacking the car up. We then jacked the front of the car
up pretty high. George was confident but I didn't like the look of it! Getting underneath
the car we disconnnected the powersteering pipes from the steering rack. This was
easiler than trying to remove the crimped connectors at the pump end. We disconnected
the powersteering pump reservoir from the bulk head. We also disconnected
the fuel lines from the fuel rail. The flexihose exhaust section could now be removed
from the cataytic converter section and the manifold removed. Secure the remaining
section of exhaust using a couple of tie clips to the exhaust support.
<P>
Removed the front wheels and plastic wheel arch protectors.
<P>
With me sitting inside the car applying the brakes George disconnected the driveshafts
which are connected using 6 bolts each. The engine was now ready to be removed.
We attached a chain to a hook on the front right of the gearbox and around the back left
engine mounting point. It's easier to lower the engine down through the front of the
engine bay than it is to lift it our since you don't need to disconnect the gearbox.
Make sure that the engine crane arm is 3/4 of way up to it's highest point when the
chain is attached. This allows you to lift the engine up slightly, pull it forward of
the subframe and then lower it.
<P>
With safety in mind the rear engine mounting point was removed first.
Then the front two and
finally the rear left. The engine was then lifted slightly, pulled forward and lowered
to the ground. It was then dragged forward by hand clear of the front cross member
such that it could be lifted back up with the engine crane to be moved out of the way.
You will find it much easier
to pull the engine forward if it's lowered onto a couple of sheets of corrigated
cardboard.
<P>
By 3pm the engine was out of the car. This included time for several cups of coffee and
breakfast. It all went very well and was probably helped by the fact that the engine
had been out of the car several times before so nothing was seized up.
<P>
Things I now need to do to prepare the car whilst a donor engine is found. Most of which
don't need to be done in essence but are a good idea whilst engine is out:
<UL>
<LI>Give the engine bay a jolly good clean.</LI>
<LI>At first sight the ignition wiring loom on the near side of the engine bay seems
    to share some plastic sheilding with the other circuits such as lighting. It
    needs to be separated out such that the ignition wiring loom can be removed.</LI>
<LI>The remaining wiring loom needs to be tidied up. I've bought some nice new coiled
    plastic conduit of various diameters replace the rather tired looking existing ones.
<LI>The nearside driveshaft was very dry and needs cleaning and regreasing/oiling.</LI>
<LI>The battery tray needs cleaning and repainting black.</LI>
<LI>A couple of paint scratches sustained during the engine extraction need to be treated.</LI>
<LI>A couple of very minor rust spots need to be treated</LI>
<LI>New slam panel bolts need to be ordered and fitted into the wings.</LI>
<LI>I'd like to remove the self leveling headlight adjuster pipes. These just
    clutter things
    up and don't work anyway now that I've lowered the car.</LI>
<LI>The immoboliser wiring needs to be disconncted from the ignition wiring loom and
    very carefully labeled for when it's spliced into the Coupe ignition wiring loom!</LI>
<LI>I managed to slightly cross thread one of wheel bolts so it will need rethreading.</LI>
<LI>The rear brakes tend to stick so I need to remove the calipers and recondition
    them.</LI>
<LI>I was having problems with the power steering so now would be a good time to
    replace the rack.</LI>
<LI>Waxoil the whole engine bay for added rust protection.</LI>
<LI>Clean and repaint the support for the radiator.</LI>
</UL>
</TD>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp;14th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
A couple of hours sat in the engine bay answered most of my wiring loom questions.
The ignition wiring loom is indeed separate from 
the rest of the wiring. The only connection between the two is a large 3 pin connector
that is attached to the same part of the loom as the connector for the ignition module.
I don't know what this is used for and I'm hoping the ignition wiring loom of the
coupe has the same connector.
<P>
The immboliser wiring is spliced into the rest of the wiring loom and is not part of the
ignition wiring loom. I may still end up rewiring it to route it better.
<P>
I removed the ECU and disconnected a couple of wires at that end. Gathered up all of
ignition wiring loom and found where it passes though the bulkhead. Not particulally
easy place to get to as the clutch pipework prevents the protective material
being pulled out of the way. I'm a bit stuck as to how to pass the cables through
the bulkhead.
I think it would be best if the ECU connector was pushed into the engine bay however
I'm not sure how to remove the rubber grommet.
<P>
Cleaned plastic battery tray and plastic wheel arch protectors. Labeled a couple more
eletrical connectors.
</TD>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp;15th</TD>

<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spent a couple of hours replacing some of the plastic sheilding around the wiring
loom. Lots more to be done but it was cold outside!


<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>May 2001</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp;15th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Just an update to let you know that no work has actually taken place on the car. This
does not mean that nothing has been happening. I have been talking to various
people about differences between fitting a 20 Valve Turbo and a 16 Valve Turbo engine.
I have also been finding out how the KEYCODE ignition system works and how the
Tipo wiring loom will need to be modified to integrate with the Coupe ignition
wiring loom. Most of this information may not been needed so I will only add it
here as needed. George has also been very busy trying to track down a good donor
Coupe of either 16VT or 20VT variety. He actually went to see a 46k mile 16VT
today but it
had taken too much of a impact at the front damaging the radiator, oil filter and
possibly also the exhaust manifold and turbo. The owner wanted too much for it
especially as the car, for an unknown reason, would not start.
The owner did not have the red
key which would prove a problem if the ECU was disconnected from the battery for
too long. This is because the red key has to be used to add blue keys to it's
list of recognised keys.
<P>
In the UK all Coupes used KEYCODE ECU, however in Yugoslavia not all Coupes had
KEYCODE ECU. If all else fails and I can't find a Coupe with a RED key
Aleksandar Medo has inquired for
me about the cost of a non KEYCODE ECU.
New they are 2300 German Marks (about &pound;725) and he is currently looking as to
how much a 2nd hand one will be.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp;16th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Thought I should better check that after the engine transplant
whether anyone would insure me and if so whether the premium would be sensible.
Whilst I the phone to <B>Direct Line</B> sorting out Eva's
insurance I asked about insuring the Tipo. I was very surprised to hear that
they can insure modified cars. When an insurance company says this they usually
mean minor modifications such as chipping, wheels or body kits.
All credit to Direct Line they said they can insure major engine modifications and even
transplants. The quote was
based on how much more power as a percentage the new engine has compared to the orginal.
I said the original was 142 bhp and I the transplanted engine would
have in the region of 220-250 bhp. This put it in the 50-75% bracket. Fully
comprehensive with full no claims discount came to a very reasonable
&pound;781 or &pound;912 protected.
</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp;18th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Found out a bit more about how the CODE immobiliser works on the Fiat Coupe. This
will be necessary in order to use the coded ECU that the donor car will come with.
The coded ECU
knocks out the ignition and fuel pump preventing the engine from starting. The
Coupe key contains a transponder which is a
passive device and does not need a battery. Around the Coupe ignition lock barrel is an
antenna. The ECU interrogates the transponder on the key using the antenna to check
it's code matches that of the ECU. There is no physical contact between the two.
I still don't
know for sure how the antenna connects to the ECU. I'm hoping its just a couple of
wires that can be made up when the whole system is fitted into the Tipo.
<P>
The
ideal solution would be to place the antenna around the Tipo ignition lock and
wire the antenna up to the Coupe ECU. By keeping the Tipo ignition lock I maintain the
steering wheel lock mechanism as the Coupe ignition lock does not fit the Tipo. 
A new Coupe style key could 
then be made containing
the transponder but cut with the Tipo ignition key pattern.
If that is not possible I would need to test how
far away from the antenna the transponder could be detected. It might be enough to
keep the Coupe key on my key ring. Another solution might be to remove the
transponder off the coupe key and permanently stick it to the antenna behind the
dashboard somehow. This is far from ideal though as I would loose the extra security
the CODE system provides.
</TD></TR>
</TABLE>


